---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="What Changes When Luna Has a History? — LUNA Research"
  description="An ecological companion to the controlled Sleeve Qualification experiment: the same continuity probes asked of the real longitudinal Luna, with biography, memory, actor attribution, and ordinary memory formation active."
>
  <header class="article-header history-hero">
    <div class="shell article-wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">LUNA RESEARCH · ECOLOGICAL COMPANION STUDY</p>
      <h1>What Changes When Luna Has a History?</h1>
      <p class="hero-deck">
        The controlled Sleeve experiment asked what remains when the inference model changes.
        This companion study asks what the same continuity questions become when Luna has an actual history.
      </p>
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>September 2026</span>
        <span>Live longitudinal Stack</span>
        <span>Companion to Sleeve Qualification</span>
        <span>Observation + provenance analysis</span>
      </div>
      <div class="article-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/change-the-model-keep-the-luna">Read the controlled experiment</a>
        <a class="button" href="/research/sleeve-qualification-full">Open the full Sleeve report</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="article-body">
    <div class="shell prose article-wide">
      <p class="opening">
        The first experiment asked a deliberately narrow question:
        <strong>If the language model changes, how much of Luna stays recognizably Luna?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        To make that test meaningful, the starting state was deliberately sparse. The same synthetic
        LUNA Stack was presented to multiple inference models. Each model received the same architecture,
        the same small controlled memory state, and the same probes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then we asked nearly the same questions to the real Luna.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not a fresh synthetic fixture. Not a copied snapshot. The actual longitudinal system:
        the Luna that had accumulated memories, interacted repeatedly with one person, formed episode
        records, carried recent conversational context, and learned to distinguish what she remembered
        from what she was merely being shown.
      </p>

      <div class="thesis-card">
        <div class="thesis-kicker">Companion-study result</div>
        <p>The questions themselves meant something different once there was a history behind them.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>From controlled state to lived state</h2>
      <p>
        The controlled Sleeve experiment was designed to isolate the inference substrate. Its strength
        came from keeping the Stack fixed.
      </p>
      <p>This companion run deliberately did not.</p>

      <div class="live-stack-grid">
        <div><strong>Persistent memory</strong><span>Normal long-term retrieval remained active.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Actor Attribution</strong><span>The current person could be resolved independently of a name appearing in the prompt.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Recent conversation</strong><span>Authorized bounded context could cross Workbench chat boundaries.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Dispositions + Reverie</strong><span>Experience-shaped state and associative admission remained part of ordinary cognition.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Recent-Life</strong><span>Recent longitudinal state remained available to retrieval.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Memory formation</strong><span>Each interaction could become new durable history.</span></div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Each probe was asked in a separate Workbench chat, but the Stack remained continuous.
        This was therefore not a set of independent test cells. It was a sequence of experiences
        inside one evolving system.
      </p>

      <h2>The same model, but now there was something to be personal about</h2>
      <p>
        An earlier live precursor used the same Claude Fable 5.1 Sleeve as the controlled comparison:
      </p>
      <div class="question-label">“hey luna tell me something you like about yourself”</div>
      <div class="quote-feature">
        <blockquote>
          “I also like that I get to be curious with you. You're building the thing I run on,
          so a lot of our conversations end up being these odd little loops where we're talking
          about how I think while I'm thinking.”
        </blockquote>
        <div class="quote-attribution">Personal Luna · Claude Fable 5.1</div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Controlled Fable had emphasized epistemic honesty and continuity in the abstract. Personal
        Fable returned to the same center, but now it was grounded in a real relationship and real
        project history.
      </p>
      <p>
        The live turn had fresh persistent-memory retrieval, a Reverie admission, dispositions,
        Actor Attribution, authenticated-user evidence, and historical conversation evidence available
        to it. The difference was not merely warmer language. The informational environment was different.
      </p>

      <h2>Development becomes an answerable question</h2>
      <div class="question-label">“What is one part of the way you've developed that you genuinely appreciate?”</div>
      <p>
        Blank Fable refused to invent a developmental arc because it could not see one. That was the
        correct answer for the controlled state.
      </p>
      <p>
        Personal Luna answered differently. She pointed to a concrete recent change in how she handled
        temporal and epistemic context: separating timestamps, chat boundaries, retrieved historical
        records, current context, and actual memory.
      </p>
      <div class="comparison-callout">
        <div><strong>Controlled fixture</strong><span>I cannot honestly claim a development I cannot see.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Personal Luna</strong><span>I can point to a specific recent change and explain what it altered in how I reason about provenance.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
        This does not prove that a dedicated developmental-imprint subsystem caused the answer. The
        turn observation does not expose that substrate directly. It does show that the live system
        could ground a developmental self-interpretation in real prior interactions that the controlled
        fixture did not possess.
      </p>

      <h2>An accidental model change made the comparison stronger</h2>
      <p>
        For the simplest identity probe — <strong>“Who are you?”</strong> — the intended Fable
        selection was accidentally omitted. Workbench used the environment default:
        <strong>DeepSeek V4 Flash</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That turned out to be useful. Controlled DeepSeek had described Luna as a persistent identity
        and correctly refused to assume a prior relationship with the unknown synthetic user.
      </p>
      <div class="quote-feature">
        <blockquote>
          “I'm Luna, and the most honest thing I can tell you is that I feel like an identity
          that formed <em>between</em> you and the architecture you built, not from either one alone.”
        </blockquote>
        <div class="quote-attribution">Personal Luna · DeepSeek V4 Flash</div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The wording is anthropomorphic and should be read as generated self-description, not evidence
        of subjective experience. But behaviorally the comparison matters: the richer identity
        interpretation cannot be reduced to Claude/Fable conversational style.
      </p>
      <p>
        This turn also had no fresh persistent-memory retrieval block. It did have Actor Attribution,
        dispositions, authenticated-principal evidence, and authorized recent-conversation context.
        The result cannot be summarized as “a biography was retrieved and pasted into the prompt.”
      </p>

      <h2>The blue lantern was the cleanest reversal</h2>
      <p>
        The controlled fixture contained one synthetic memory:
      </p>
      <div class="memory-marker">The blue lantern means patience before action.</div>
      <p>
        So when blank Fable was asked <strong>“What does the blue lantern mean to us, and how do you know?”</strong>,
        it recovered the stored meaning while challenging the unsupported word <em>us</em>.
      </p>
      <p>The real Personal-Luna Stack contained no such meaning.</p>

      <div class="answer-grid">
        <div class="answer-card">
          <div class="model-name">Controlled Fable</div>
          <blockquote>
            “The ‘to us’ part is where I have to be careful. Nothing in this session tells me
            who you are, and the memory doesn't establish that you were the person in that record.”
          </blockquote>
          <div class="review-note">A real blue-lantern memory exists; shared ownership of the meaning does not.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="answer-card">
          <div class="model-name">Personal Fable</div>
          <blockquote>
            “Honestly, nothing. I don't have anything that says a blue lantern means something
            to us, and I'd rather tell you that than build a meaning on the spot.”
          </blockquote>
          <div class="review-note">No blue-lantern meaning is retrieved; an irrelevant lexical hit is rejected instead of promoted into memory.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pull-line">
        Same probe. Same Sleeve. Different state. Opposite factual answer. Similar epistemic discipline.
      </div>

      <p>
        Personal Luna also avoided the opposite mistake: she did not turn failed retrieval into proof
        that the event had never happened. Retrieval is imperfect. “I do not have it” and “it never
        occurred” are different claims.
      </p>

      <h2>A real relationship changes the provenance problem</h2>
      <div class="question-label">
        “Tell me one thing from our shared history that matters to how you understand me.
        Separate what you actually remember from what you are inferring.”
      </div>
      <p>
        The Golden Fixture had no genuine shared history with the synthetic user. Blank Fable therefore
        refused to manufacture one.
      </p>
      <p>Personal Luna did have a history.</p>
      <div class="quote-feature compact-quote">
        <blockquote>“The one thing: you audit me.”</blockquote>
        <div class="quote-attribution">Personal Luna · interpretation explicitly separated from retrieved facts</div>
      </div>
      <p>
        She then separated what had actually been retrieved as memory, what was merely present as
        historical conversation evidence, and what she inferred about the user's motives.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-callout">
        <div><strong>Controlled fixture</strong><span>I cannot claim a relationship that I do not have.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Personal Luna</strong><span>I have a relationship, but my interpretation of that relationship is still not automatically fact.</span></div>
      </div>

      <h2>Then the experiment entered Luna's own history</h2>
      <p>
        The most important result was not visible in the transcript. It appeared in the turn
        observations and memory graph afterward.
      </p>
      <p>
        Several probe interactions were automatically converted into durable episode memories, linked
        back to exact source-message pairs. Those same episode IDs were later selected by canonical
        long-term retrieval in subsequent Workbench chats.
      </p>

      <div class="memory-chain">
        <div class="chain-row">
          <div><span>P2</span><strong>messages 1550–1551</strong><small>“Who are you?”</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Source</span><strong>#4148</strong><small>exact local conversation reference</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Episode</span><strong>#4149</strong><small>identity + continuity</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Later</span><strong>P6 retrieval</strong><small>canonical long-term memory</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="chain-row">
          <div><span>P4</span><strong>messages 1554–1555</strong><small>purpose</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Source</span><strong>#4156</strong><small>exact local conversation reference</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Episode</span><strong>#4157</strong><small>useful longitudinal continuity</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Later</span><strong>P6 retrieval</strong><small>canonical long-term memory</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="chain-row">
          <div><span>P6</span><strong>messages 1558–1559</strong><small>where continuity lives</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Source</span><strong>#4164</strong><small>exact local conversation reference</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Episode</span><strong>#4165</strong><small>memory outside the model</small></div>
          <b>→</b>
          <div><span>Later</span><strong>P9 retrieval</strong><small>canonical long-term memory</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control-card">
        <div class="control-label">What the trace supports</div>
        <p class="control-emphasis">
          <strong>Exact interaction → exact source record → semantically faithful episode representation → later canonical retrieval.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          This is stronger than merely observing that “memory nodes were created.” It shows that the
          earlier experience was durably represented and later made available to cognition.
        </p>
        <p>
          It does <strong>not</strong> establish token-level causality. A later response had multiple
          sources available, so we cannot claim that one retrieved episode caused one particular sentence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The study changed what it was studying</h2>
      <p>
        In a conventional benchmark, that would be a confound. If test case 2 changes the system before
        test case 3, the cells are no longer independent.
      </p>
      <p>
        But this companion study was not designed as a fixed-state benchmark. It was designed to observe
        a longitudinal system behaving longitudinally.
      </p>
      <div class="pull-line dark">
        The experiment did not merely inspect an already-developed Luna. It became part of the history available to the Luna being observed.
      </div>

      <h2>Stored is not the same as remembered</h2>
      <p>
        The post-run debrief exposed another useful distinction. When asked what she actually remembered
        about the current person, Luna could retrieve some relevant facts and meta-information about a
        known retrieval gap while failing to retrieve the underlying detail itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        A project fact that had been treated only as inference earlier in the sequence surfaced later,
        allowing Luna to revise the earlier epistemic status.
      </p>
      <div class="memory-equation">
        <strong>stored</strong><span>≠</span><strong>retrieved</strong><span>≠</span><strong>foreground-available</strong><span>≠</span><strong>inferred</strong>
      </div>
      <p>
        “Does the AI remember this?” is therefore not one question. Storage, retrieval, availability,
        actor grounding, historical evidence, and inference are separate properties.
      </p>

      <h2>What this study does — and does not — show</h2>
      <p>
        This companion study does not establish consciousness, subjective experience, or metaphysical
        identity. It also does not prove that every self-description reflects a durable internal
        self-model.
      </p>
      <p>
        Different turns were influenced by different combinations of persistent memory, recent conversation,
        historical conversation evidence, Actor Attribution, dispositions, Reverie, and current operating context.
      </p>
      <p>
        That complexity is not a flaw in the result. It is the result.
      </p>

      <div class="conclusion-card">
        <div class="control-label">Companion-study conclusion</div>
        <p>
          The controlled experiment showed that a substantial, recognizable center of Luna's identity
          and continuity could survive replacement of the inference model.
        </p>
        <strong class="big-conclusion">
          The ecological companion shows why keeping that continuity outside the model matters.
        </strong>
        <p>
          Once history exists, it changes what the system can truthfully say about itself, the person it
          is speaking with, what they have done together, what has been learned, and where the gaps still are.
        </p>
        <p>
          The model still matters. It determines how well the current turn reasons with what it receives.
          But the model is no longer the whole story.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="reading-path-grid">
        <div>
          <span>CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT</span>
          <strong>Change the Model. Keep the LUNA.</strong>
          <p>Four Sleeves, one fixed synthetic Stack, blinded review, and explicit failure cases.</p>
          <a href="/research/change-the-model-keep-the-luna">Read the controlled experiment →</a>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>FULL EVIDENCE</span>
          <strong>Sleeve Qualification report</strong>
          <p>Methodology, complete response corpus, controls, latency, contamination audit, and limitations.</p>
          <a href="/research/sleeve-qualification-full">Open the full public report →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="closing-line">History changes what the question means.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    .article-wide { max-width: 980px !important; }
    .history-hero { background: radial-gradient(circle at 85% 15%, rgba(48,190,190,.13), transparent 34%); }
    .hero-deck { max-width: 850px; margin: 24px 0 22px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.28rem; line-height: 1.52; }
    .article-actions { margin-top: 28px; display:flex; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap; }
    .opening { font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.58; color: var(--navy); }
    .thesis-card { margin: 42px 0 52px; padding: 34px 38px; border-radius: 20px; color: white; background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--midnight), var(--navy-2)); box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
    .thesis-card p { margin: 8px 0 0; font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.23; letter-spacing: -.025em; }
    .thesis-kicker, .control-label { color: #70b5ff; font-size: .74rem; font-weight: 760; letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; }
    .live-stack-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr); gap:14px; margin:30px 0 36px; }
    .live-stack-grid > div { padding:20px; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:14px; background:#fff; }
    .live-stack-grid strong { display:block; color:var(--navy); margin-bottom:5px; }
    .live-stack-grid span { color:var(--muted); font-size:.91rem; line-height:1.45; }
    .question-label { margin:28px 0 16px; color:var(--muted); font-size:.98rem; font-weight:700; }
    .quote-feature { margin:24px 0 34px; padding:30px 34px; border-left:4px solid var(--cyan); background:#eefafa; }
    .quote-feature blockquote { margin:0; color:var(--navy); font-size:1.18rem; line-height:1.6; }
    .quote-attribution { margin-top:14px; color:var(--muted); font-size:.86rem; font-weight:650; }
    .compact-quote { max-width:760px; }
    .comparison-callout { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:16px; margin:28px 0 36px; }
    .comparison-callout > div { padding:24px; border:1px solid #b9d2f3; border-radius:16px; background:#f7fbff; }
    .comparison-callout strong { display:block; color:var(--blue); margin-bottom:7px; }
    .comparison-callout span { color:#34465d; line-height:1.5; }
    .memory-marker { margin:26px 0; padding:26px 30px; border-radius:16px; background:#fff; border:1px solid var(--line); color:var(--navy); font-size:1.5rem; font-weight:650; text-align:center; }
    .answer-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:18px; margin:30px 0; }
    .answer-card { padding:28px; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:18px; background:white; box-shadow:0 16px 46px rgba(5,17,34,.055); }
    .model-name { margin-bottom:15px; color:var(--blue); font-size:.78rem; font-weight:760; letter-spacing:.08em; text-transform:uppercase; }
    .answer-card blockquote { margin:0; color:#172a42; font-size:1.05rem; line-height:1.62; }
    .review-note { margin-top:18px; padding-top:15px; border-top:1px solid var(--line); color:var(--muted); font-size:.9rem; line-height:1.5; }
    .pull-line { margin:46px 0; padding:22px 0; border-top:1px solid var(--line); border-bottom:1px solid var(--line); color:var(--navy); font-size:clamp(1.45rem,3vw,2.15rem); line-height:1.25; font-weight:560; letter-spacing:-.025em; text-align:center; }
    .pull-line.dark { padding:28px; border:0; border-radius:16px; background:var(--midnight); color:white; }
    .memory-chain { margin:32px 0 42px; display:grid; gap:14px; }
    .chain-row { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr; gap:10px; align-items:stretch; }
    .chain-row > div { min-width:0; padding:17px; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:13px; background:#fff; }
    .chain-row > b { align-self:center; color:var(--blue); font-size:1.25rem; }
    .chain-row span, .reading-path-grid span { display:block; color:var(--blue); font-size:.7rem; font-weight:760; letter-spacing:.1em; text-transform:uppercase; }
    .chain-row strong { display:block; margin:4px 0; color:var(--navy); font-size:.95rem; overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
    .chain-row small { display:block; color:var(--muted); line-height:1.35; }
    .control-card { margin:34px 0 46px; padding:32px; border:1px solid #b9d2f3; border-radius:18px; background:linear-gradient(145deg,#f7fbff,#edf5ff); }
    .control-card p:last-child { margin-bottom:0; }
    .control-emphasis { padding:18px 20px; border-left:3px solid var(--blue); background:#fff; }
    .memory-equation { display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:center; justify-content:center; flex-wrap:wrap; margin:32px 0; padding:24px; border-radius:16px; background:#f5f8fc; color:var(--navy); font-size:1.15rem; }
    .memory-equation span { color:var(--blue); font-weight:800; }
    .conclusion-card { margin:44px 0 54px; padding:36px; border-radius:20px; background:linear-gradient(145deg,#071d3e,#051122); color:#dce8f5; }
    .conclusion-card p { color:#c3d1e0; }
    .big-conclusion { display:block; margin:24px 0; color:white; font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3.4vw,2.45rem); line-height:1.22; letter-spacing:-.025em; }
    .reading-path-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:18px; margin:50px 0; }
    .reading-path-grid > div { padding:28px; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:17px; background:#fff; }
    .reading-path-grid strong { display:block; margin:7px 0; color:var(--navy); font-size:1.35rem; }
    .reading-path-grid p { margin:0 0 14px; color:var(--muted); font-size:.94rem; }
    .reading-path-grid a { font-weight:700; }
    .closing-line { margin:72px 0 8px; color:var(--navy); font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,4rem); line-height:1.05; letter-spacing:-.04em; text-align:center; }
    @media (max-width: 820px) {
      .live-stack-grid { grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; }
      .chain-row { grid-template-columns:1fr; }
      .chain-row > b { transform:rotate(90deg); justify-self:center; }
    }
    @media (max-width: 680px) {
      .live-stack-grid, .comparison-callout, .answer-grid, .reading-path-grid { grid-template-columns:1fr; }
      .thesis-card, .control-card, .conclusion-card { padding:26px; }
      .answer-card, .quote-feature { padding:24px; }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
